      "explain_why_timing_must_match_compress": "Explain why timing must match compression and fuel.",
      "battery-test_detail": "Cold weather doubles cranking load while cutting battery output. A weak battery in November is a dead battery in February. Free at NAPA / O'Reilly / AutoZone.",
      "tires-check_detail": "Below 4/32\" tread = unsafe in slush. Below 2/32\" = illegal. Cold drops tire pressure ~1 psi per 10°F drop — a 70°F to 0°F swing drops pressure 7 psi.",
      "antifreeze_detail": "A coolant tester (hydrometer or refractometer, $5–15) tells you the freeze point. Should protect to at least -34°F. Old / contaminated coolant freezes higher.",
      "wiper-fluid_detail": "Standard summer washer fluid freezes around 32°F. In Maine, that fluid sits in the reservoir frozen for 4 months — and shatters the reservoir if it expands.",
      "wipers_detail": "Summer-worn blades chatter on first slush storm. Full-frame \"winter blades\" handle ice better than sleek beam blades.",
      "block-heater_detail": "Block heater = a small heating element in the engine block, plugged in overnight. Warms oil + coolant for easier cold start. Many Maine homes have an outlet near the driveway for exactly this.",
      "oil-grade_detail": "Below 0°F, conventional oil thickens and starves the engine on cold start. Most modern cars spec 0W-20 or 5W-30 full-synthetic — verify yours and switch to synthetic if you're not already.",
      "belts-hoses_detail": "Cold makes rubber stiffer. A cracked serpentine belt that's been \"fine\" all summer can snap on the first -10°F morning. Hoses can burst.",
      "emergency-kit_detail": "Maine rural drivers can be stranded in below-zero temps for hours waiting on AAA or a tow. The trunk kit is genuine survival equipment.",
      "door-locks_detail": "Frozen door locks at 6am on a Tuesday is the most Maine-of-Maine moments. Graphite lock lubricant (NOT oil — oil collects ice).",
      "windshield-wash_detail": "A small chip in November becomes a coast-to-coast crack on the first -20°F morning when you turn the defroster on. Thermal shock is real.",
      "undercarriage-rinse_detail": "After winter, undercarriage is caked with salt brine. Rinse off ASAP and inspect for new rust scaling on brake/fuel lines, frame, exhaust.",
      "lab-misfire_intro": "Customer drives in a 2014 Toyota Camry. \"Check-engine light is FLASHING and the car shakes hard at stoplights. Started this morning.\"",
      "lab-no-start_intro": "Customer text: \"Won't start. Just clicks. -5°F here in Caribou.\"",
      "lab-brakes_intro": "Customer: \"Pedal shakes hard when I brake from highway speed. Started after winter — fine in spring.\"",
      "lab-overheat_intro": "Customer: \"Temp gauge climbs to red when stopped at lights, drops when I start moving. Highway is fine.\"",
      "lab-noise_intro": "Customer: \"Humming sound that gets louder above 40 mph. Worse turning right than left.\"",
      "lab-leak_intro": "Customer: \"Sweet smell inside the car when the heat is on. Coolant level keeps dropping. No puddle in driveway.\"",
      "no-start_intro": "Pick the symptom that matches what your car is doing right now.",
      "misfire_intro": "Engine is running rough, hesitating, or shaking. Walk through what you're feeling.",
