      "thank_you_for_exploring_cephalopod_bio": "Thank you for exploring cephalopod biology",
      "cephalopods_are_among_the_most_remarka": "Cephalopods are among the most remarkable animals on Earth. They evolved intelligence + camouflage + tool use + senescence-controlled lifespans from a starting point as different from our own as you can imagine. They're proof that there are many ways to be a complex, sentient creature — and that the universe of biology is bigger than any one branch of the evolutionary tree.\n\nThis simulator was built to help students see what's remarkable. The 12 playable species + 80+ tabs of content + 100+ biology facts + dozens of lesson plans are all in service of one goal: helping students fall in love with biology by meeting these animals on their own terms.\n\nThe science is real. Every claim has a citation. Every researcher named is doing real work. The welfare debates are happening right now in real policy + scientific communities. The threats to cephalopod populations are real. The opportunities for student citizen science are real.\n\nIf you're a teacher: thank you for using this tool. We hope your students engage deeply + carry their curiosity beyond the classroom.\n\nIf you're a student: thank you for being curious. The world needs people who pay attention to the strange + beautiful creatures sharing our planet.\n\nIf you're a parent: thank you for supporting your child's science learning. The conversations you have at the dinner table about cephalopod welfare or octopus farming are part of how your child becomes a scientifically + ethically literate adult.\n\nIf you're a researcher: thank you for the work that makes this possible. Every claim in this simulator stands on your shoulders.\n\nIf you're from a coastal community: thank you for the traditional knowledge of cephalopod biology that often predates academic science by centuries. We hope this tool respects + represents that knowledge alongside Western scientific literature.\n\nIf you're from an Indigenous community: thank you for the cultural narratives + ecological wisdom that this tool draws on. Many cephalopod biology insights — about respect for animal life, about the interconnection of ocean ecosystems, about the limits of human knowledge — have deep cultural roots.\n\nThe simulator is FREE + will remain free. No login. No data collection. No FERPA surface. All settings local. If you find errors, please report them. If you have suggestions, please share them.\n\nFor more information + updates, visit alloflow.com.\n\nGo deeper. Be curious. Stay connected to the natural world. And remember: cephalopods have been here for 500 million years. They've survived all five mass extinctions. They're still finding new ways to thrive. There's much to learn from them.\n\n— The AlloFlow Cephalopod Lab Team, 2026",
      "chromatophore_color_study": "Chromatophore color study",
      "draw_an_octopus_body_use_five_differen": "Draw an octopus body. Use FIVE different colored pencils or markers to represent the different chromatophore types (yellow, red, brown, black, plus blue for iridophore). Then create a \"camouflage scene\" where the octopus matches its surroundings.",
      "sucker_cup_biomechanics": "Sucker-cup biomechanics",
      "design_build_a_simple_sucker_mechanism": "Design + build a simple sucker mechanism using clay, balloons, or 3D printing. Test it on different surfaces (wet, rough, smooth). Compare to octopus sucker design.",
      "bioluminescence_photophore_art": "Bioluminescence + photophore art",
      "create_a_deep_sea_scene_using_black_pa": "Create a deep-sea scene using black paper + glow-in-the-dark paint or markers. Include bioluminescent creatures: vampire squid, bobtail squid, anglerfish. Layer photophore patterns.",
      "field_journal_illustration": "Field journal illustration",
      "keep_a_daily_cephalopod_observation_jo": "Keep a daily cephalopod observation journal. Each entry: sketch one cephalopod from the simulator + write 2-3 sentences of observation in scientific style.",
