      "you_re_curious_about_a_term_you_open_t": "You're curious about a term. You open the Glossary. You spend 20 minutes reading entries, each with citations.",
      "you_photograph_an_octopus_at_the_aquar": "You photograph an octopus at the aquarium. You submit to iNaturalist. Your data is now in the global database.",
      "cephalopod_biology_is_among_the_riches": "Cephalopod biology is among the richest entry points into modern biology, ecology, evolution, ethics, and cognition. They're ALIEN ENOUGH to provoke curiosity (no bones, blue blood, color-blind color-matching, distributed intelligence) but FAMILIAR ENOUGH to engage emotional connection (intelligence, individual personality, sentience). They're short-lived but their populations are growing under climate change — making them BOTH a curiosity AND a real ecological story. The science is genuinely OPEN — questions remain, debates are active, and student curiosity can engage with REAL unresolved problems. We are not just teaching facts; we are inviting students into a working scientific community.\n\nFor middle school: cephalopod camouflage is a perfect entry into adaptive evolution. The \"color-blind animal that matches color\" puzzle is a real biological mystery that students can engage with.\n\nFor high school + AP Bio: cephalopods raise convergent evolution, RNA editing, semelparity, ethics, climate science, and biomedicine in a single integrated package.\n\nFor elementary: cephalopods are EMOTIONAL — students bond with animals they meet through the simulator. This builds long-term engagement with science as a whole.",
      "the_simulator_is_a_tool_not_a_curricul": "The simulator is a tool, not a curriculum. Pair it with reading, discussion, writing, and reflection. The most effective uses I've seen:\n\n(1) HOOK — open with the simulator. Project on big screen. Let students see camouflage in action before any vocabulary. Then introduce vocabulary as their CURIOSITY demands it.\n\n(2) EVIDENCE — use the simulator to GENERATE DATA students can analyze. Multi-trial runs + data tables + class comparisons.\n\n(3) DISCUSSION — use the simulator as a SHARED REFERENCE for class discussions. \"Remember when the moray eel attacked? What did the octopus do? Why might that work?\"\n\n(4) ASSESSMENT — use the simulator as an authentic performance task. Students can demonstrate understanding by what they DO in the simulator, not just what they write.\n\n(5) REFLECTION — close with the simulator. After 2 weeks of study, ask students to predict what would happen in different scenarios. Test their predictions.",
      "students_who_lose_interest_try_the_qui": "Students who lose interest: try the Quiz tab. Self-paced + immediate feedback works for many disengaged students.\n\nStudents overwhelmed by content: simplify. Use the Glossary + Vocabulary tabs as scaffolding. Don't need to cover everything in 2 weeks.\n\nStudents skeptical of \"interesting facts\": connect to primary research. Show the citations. Trace claims back to peer-reviewed papers. This builds science literacy.\n\nStudents resistant to ethics topics: be patient. Use the Debate Prompts to depersonalize. Take a position THEY don't hold, then defend it. This builds empathy + understanding.\n\nStudents from different backgrounds: emphasize SHARED human curiosity. Cephalopods are interesting to people from every culture + every economic background. The Cultural Refs tab can help.\n\nStudents asking \"but what's on the TEST?\" — direct them to the Exam Prep tab. Let them see what's being assessed + why.",
      "cephalopod_biology_connects_to_nearly_": "Cephalopod biology connects to nearly every other school subject. The Cross-disciplinary tab maps these connections. Some that work well:\n\nMath: stats analysis of trial data (Sample Data Sets). Population modeling.\n\nELA: Sy Montgomery + Godfrey-Smith books. Reading scientific abstracts. Position papers.\n\nSocial studies: history of cephalopod cultural depictions. Welfare legislation across countries.\n\nArt: Hokusai. Iridophore patterns. Field-note illustration.\n\nComputer science: simulation design. Distributed cognition algorithms.\n\nEngineering: biomimicry (soft robotics, suction adhesives).\n\nHealth: tetrodotoxin medications. Squid axon in cardiology + neurology.\n\nEthics: sentience recognition + animal welfare.",
      "this_tool_was_built_by_aaron_pomeranz_": "This tool was built by Aaron Pomeranz (PsyD, school psychologist) at Portland Public Schools, working at King Middle School — an EL Education school in Portland, Maine. It is designed for the EL Education model: students-as-scientists, crew + critique + reflection, evidence-based reasoning, ethical engagement.\n\nThe tool is provided FREE. No login. No data collection. No FERPA surface. Browser-based + localStorage-only.\n\nIt was built because cephalopod biology is the perfect entry into modern biology + ecology + ethics — and existing curricular resources don't adequately cover it. School science textbooks devote 1-2 pages to cephalopods. Documentaries are excellent but expensive. Aquarium visits are time-limited.\n\nThis simulator makes cephalopod biology engaging, accessible, and pedagogically deep. It's not a replacement for hands-on field experience or aquarium visits. It IS a substantial supplement that any teacher can use.\n\nIf you're using this tool: please share your experiences. Email aaron@allofow.com or use the feedback link in the resource tab. The tool will continue to develop based on classroom use.",
      "planned_additions_to_the_simulator_202": "Planned additions to the simulator (2026+):\n\n• Multiplayer mode (students collaborate on simulator missions)\n• Voice-over for accessibility\n• Languages other than English (Spanish, Portuguese, Mandarin priority)\n• Local-content authoring (teachers add their own scenarios)\n• Integration with iNaturalist + REEF citizen-science workflows\n• Better progress tracking + portfolio export\n• AI-assisted comprehension checking\n• More cephalopod species (especially deep-sea + cold-water species)\n• Better mobile / tablet support\n\nIf you're a developer or designer + want to contribute, please reach out.",
      "this_simulator_was_built_in_2025_2026_": "This simulator was built in 2025-2026 by Aaron Pomeranz, drawing on the published work of dozens of cephalopod biologists. Specific thanks to:\n\n• Jennifer Mather (University of Lethbridge) — foundational welfare + cognition research\n• Roger Hanlon (MBL Woods Hole) — camouflage + display behavior\n• Bret Grasse (MBL Woods Hole) — cephalopod husbandry\n• Sy Montgomery — public engagement\n• Z. Yan Wang (UWa) — optic gland research\n• Robyn Crook (SFSU) — welfare + pain research\n• Jonathan Birch (LSE) — sentience review + policy\n• Alexandra Schnell (Cambridge) — cuttlefish cognition\n• The MBARI team — deep-sea cephalopod research\n• The Birch et al. 2021 LSE report — foundational welfare evidence\n\nAll cited research is publicly available. Every claim in the simulator can be traced back to peer-reviewed publication. The tool is built on the shoulders of these researchers + many others.\n\nIf you find errors or have suggestions, please email aaron@alloflow.com.",
