      "show_one_of_these_myths_to_students_be": "Show one of these myths to students BEFORE introducing the science. Have them rate confidence (1-5) on the claim. Reveal the correction. Discuss: how could you have detected the myth? What kind of source would you trust?",
      "evidence_based_correction": "✓ Evidence-based correction: ",
      "how_to_use": "📝 How to use",
      "watch_a_cephalopod_sim_aquarium_or_wil": "Watch a cephalopod (sim, aquarium, or wild). Every 30 seconds, record the most prominent behavior from the list. Sum across a 20-min observation = a behavioral budget. Compare across individuals or contexts.",
      "why_this_matters_5": "🎧 Why this matters",
      "the_ocean_is_not_silent_sounds_are_cri": "The ocean is NOT silent. Sounds are critical for many marine species — from whale communication to dolphin echolocation to fish spawning calls. Notably, cephalopods themselves are LARGELY DEAF in the vertebrate sense; they detect particle motion in water rather than pressure waves. This means SOUND is mostly a vertebrate-marine channel.",
      "why_this_matters_6": "🌟 Why this matters",
      "citizen_science_is_real_science_heberl": "Citizen science is REAL science. Heberling et al. 2021 (Front. Ecol. Environ.) found that citizen-science contributions have fueled 47+ cephalopod range-shift papers since 2020 alone. Your observations matter — and you don't need a degree to contribute.",
