      "bat_echolocation_at_source_up_to_140_d": "Bat echolocation at source (up to 140 dB!)",
      "blue_whale_call_loudest_animal": "Blue whale call (loudest animal)",
      "decibels_are_logarithmic_every_10_db_i": "Decibels are logarithmic: every +10 dB is perceived as roughly twice as loud. A bat's echolocation call at the source (140 dB) would be painfully loud to humans — but we can't hear it because it's ultrasonic!",
      "bioacoustics_explorer": "🔊 Bioacoustics Explorer",
      "play": "🔊 Play",
      "convergent_evolution": "🧬 Convergent Evolution",
      "both_bats_and_dolphins_evolved_echoloc": "Both bats and dolphins evolved echolocation independently — a textbook case of convergent evolution! Despite being separated by 80 million years of evolution, they use remarkably similar strategies: emit high-frequency pulses, listen for echoes, and build a \"sound picture\" of their environment. Even the neural circuits that process echoes are similar, suggesting evolution has found an optimal solution to the sonar problem.",
      "cricket_thermometer_dolbear_s_law": "Cricket Thermometer — Dolbear's Law",
      "crickets_are_cold_blooded_so_their_chi": "Crickets are cold-blooded, so their chirp rate changes with temperature. Amos Dolbear discovered this relationship in 1897. Count the number of chirps in 14 seconds and add 40 to get the approximate temperature in °F!",
      "t_f_n_40": "T(°F) = N₁₄ + 40",
      "where_n_number_of_chirps_in_14_seconds": "where N₁₄ = number of chirps in 14 seconds",
      "fahrenheit": "Fahrenheit",
      "celsius": "Celsius",
      "the_physics_cricket_muscles_contract_f": "The physics: cricket muscles contract faster in warm temperatures because chemical reactions speed up. This is the same reason reptiles are sluggish in cold weather — enzymes work slower at lower temperatures (Arrhenius equation).",
      "echolocation_across_species": "🔍 Echolocation Across Species",
      "multiple_animals_have_evolved_the_abil": "Multiple animals have evolved the ability to \"see\" with sound. Compare their sonar systems:",
      "comparison_of_echolocation_across_spec": "Comparison of echolocation across species",
      "note_sound_travels_4_3x_faster_in_wate": "Note: Sound travels ~4.3x faster in water than air, so dolphin sonar has much greater range than bat sonar at similar frequencies.",
      "speed_of_sound_in_different_media": "⚡ Speed of Sound in Different Media",
      "sound_moves_slowly_through_air_because": "Sound moves slowly through air because air molecules are far apart. Speed increases ~0.6 m/s per °C rise in temperature.",
      "water_molecules_are_closer_together_tr": "Water molecules are closer together, transmitting vibrations much faster. This is why dolphin sonar works at much greater distances than bat sonar.",
      "rigid_solids_transmit_sound_fastest_be": "Rigid solids transmit sound fastest because atoms are tightly packed and bonded. You can hear a train coming by putting your ear to the rail long before you hear it through air!",
      "nature_vs_technology_sonar_showdown": "🤖 Nature vs Technology: Sonar Showdown",
      "bat_echolocation": "Bat Echolocation",
      "naval_sonar_2": "Naval SONAR",
      "pound_for_pound_bat_echolocation_is_th": "Pound for pound, bat echolocation is the most sophisticated sonar system on Earth. Engineers study bats to improve robot navigation and autonomous vehicles.",
      "ai_sound_map_generator": "AI Sound Map Generator",
