      "grayWolf_desc": "Locally extirpated in Maine for over a century. Reintroduction is the most dramatic conservation move possible: documented in Yellowstone to trigger a trophic cascade by reducing elk browse, letting streamside willows and aspen recover. (Whether wolves also reshaped stream channels themselves is still debated among scientists.)",
      "beaver_desc": "The defining engineer of New England wetlands. Their dams raise water tables, slow flooding, create wet meadows, and create firebreaks. Recovering from near-extinction in the 1800s fur trade.",
      "moose_desc": "Maine's iconic megaherbivore and a key prey species for any returning wolf population. Numbers have declined sharply since 2014 due to winter ticks, an outbreak driven by milder winters under climate change.",
      "deer_desc": "Hyperabundant in much of southern New England since predators were removed. Heavy browse pressure prevents oak and pine regeneration. In Maine the picture is mixed: dense in the south and along the coast, sparse in the deep north woods.",
      "salmon_desc": "Critically endangered in the Gulf of Maine. The Penobscot River is the only Maine river with consistent returning runs. Dam removal has been the key conservation lever; in 2012 and 2013 the Veazie and Great Works dams came down through the Penobscot River Restoration Project.",
