      "core_path_blurb": "Start with recognition and emergency activation, then learn the core response modules.",
      "more_practice": "More practice, accessibility, mastery, and resources",
      "depth_adult_child_infant": "Depth: at least 2 in (5 cm) and no more than about 2.4 in (6 cm) for an adult; about 2 in (5 cm) for a child; about 1.5 in (4 cm) for an infant — in each case roughly one third of the depth of the chest. Source: AHA 2020 Guidelines for CPR & ECC.",
      "b3d_back": "← Menu",
      "b3d_title": "Body position in 3D",
      "b3d_intro": "The rhythm trainer teaches you how FAST. This teaches you WHERE — where your hands go on a real chest, where the AED pads go, and what the recovery position actually looks like from every side. Those are the things a flat diagram teaches worst.",
      "b3d_scope_lead": "Scope: ",
      "b3d_scope": "lay rescuer, compression-focused, covering adult, child and infant. This is orientation and practice, not certification — it cannot tell you whether your depth is real, which is exactly what an instructor with a manikin can. Take a hands-on course. In a real emergency, call 911 first or send someone to.",
      "b3d_tab_gate": "1 · Which one do they need?",
      "b3d_tab_place": "2 · Hand placement",
      "b3d_tab_depth": "3 · Depth + recoil",
      "b3d_tab_aed": "4 · AED pads",
      "b3d_tab_recovery": "5 · Recovery position",
      "b3d_tab_call": "6 · Run the call",
      "b3d_age": "Who is it?",
      "b3d_viewer_label": "Body diagram, 3D. Interactive. Arrow keys rotate, plus and minus zoom, zero resets. Every target here is also a button below.",
      "b3d_failed": "3D view unavailable on this device or network. Every target and every step is a button below — nothing here needs the picture.",
      "b3d_loading": "Loading the body diagram…",
      "b3d_hint": "Schematic on purpose — a diagram you can walk around, not an anatomical model. Drag or arrow keys to spin.",
      "b3d_gate_h": "Before anything else: are they breathing normally?",
      "b3d_gate_p": "This single question decides everything that follows. Getting it backwards is the most consequential mistake in this whole module.",
      "b3d_gate_cpr": "→ Compressions",
      "b3d_gate_rec": "→ Recovery position",
      "b3d_never": "Never correct",
      "b3d_place_h": "Where do your hands go?",
      "b3d_place_p": "Tap a spot on the chest in the 3D view, or pick from the list. Both do the same thing.",
      "b3d_aed_h": "Where do the AED pads go?",
      "b3d_aed_p": "The pads carry a picture showing this, and the AED talks you through it. Knowing the shape in advance means the picture makes sense when you are under pressure. Two pads, diagonally opposite, so the heart sits between them.",
      "b3d_aed_rules": "Before you press the button",
      "b3d_depth_h": "How hard, and what happens between pushes",
      "b3d_depth_for": " — depth for this age",
      "b3d_breaths": "Do breaths matter here?",
      "b3d_rate": "And the rate",
      "b3d_rate_body": "100 to 120 compressions a minute for every age, which is faster than most people expect. The CPR + AED module has a rhythm trainer for exactly this. Keep interruptions as short as you can, and if an AED arrives, turn it on and do what it says.",
      "b3d_call_h": "Run the call",
      "b3d_call_p": "The other tabs each teach one piece. Here you have to put them together the way a real call makes you — assess, choose a technique for the person in front of you, place your hands, and adapt when an AED turns up.",
      "b3d_call_back": "← Scenarios",
      "b3d_call_step": "Step ",
      "b3d_call_unsafe": "Unsafe. ",
      "b3d_call_finish": "See how you did →",
      "b3d_call_next": "Next →",
      "b3d_call_grade": "Grade: ",
      "b3d_call_wrongs": " wrong · ",
      "b3d_call_unsafes": " unsafe",
      "b3d_call_unsafe_h": "The unsafe choices matter more than the grade",
      "b3d_call_unsafe_b": "Every unsafe option in this scenario is one people really choose, usually while trying to help. Run it again and notice what made it look reasonable at the time.",
      "b3d_call_debrief": "What this call teaches",
      "b3d_call_again": "↺ Run it again",
      "b3d_call_next_case": "Another scenario →",
      "b3d_rec_h": "Recovery position, in order",
      "b3d_rec_p": "Only for someone unresponsive who IS breathing normally. Work through it and watch the body turn.",
      "b3d_rec_reset": "↺ Start again",
      "b3d_rec_done": "Positioned — now keep watching",
      "b3d_rec_done_body": "The recovery position buys a protected airway; it does not end the emergency. Stay with them, keep checking that breathing is still normal, and if it stops or turns to gasping, roll them onto their back and start compressions straight away.",
      "hypothesis_input": "First-aid readiness hypothesis",
