      "capitals_desc": "Capitals only — can you get all 20?",
      "landmarks_desc": "Fly over the world's most famous natural wonders!",
      "lift_content": "Lift is generated by the shape of the wing (airfoil). Air moves faster over the curved top surface, creating lower pressure above the wing than below. This pressure difference pushes the wing UP. Lift increases with speed² — double your speed, quadruple your lift!",
      "drag_content": "Drag opposes motion through the air. There are two types: parasite drag (from the shape of the aircraft pushing through air) increases with speed², and induced drag (a byproduct of creating lift) decreases with speed. Together they create a 'drag bucket' — an optimal speed where total drag is minimized.",
      "thrust_content": "Thrust is produced by engines — either propellers (which are spinning airfoils) or jet engines (which accelerate air backward, pushing the plane forward via Newton's Third Law). More thrust = more acceleration. In level flight, thrust exactly equals drag.",
      "weight_content": "In straight, level, unaccelerated flight, four forces are in perfect balance: Lift = Weight, and Thrust = Drag. When you pitch up, you trade speed for altitude (kinetic energy → potential energy). When you pitch down, you trade altitude for speed. This is conservation of energy in action!",
      "bernoulli_content": "Daniel Bernoulli discovered that as a fluid (including air) speeds up, its pressure drops. This is why airplane wings work: the curved upper surface makes air travel faster, reducing pressure on top. The higher pressure underneath pushes the wing up. You can demonstrate this by blowing over a piece of paper — it rises!",
      "navigation_content": "Modern navigation uses GPS, but the fundamentals haven't changed: heading (compass direction), airspeed, altitude, and time. Great circle routes (the shortest path between two points on a sphere) appear curved on flat maps but are actually straight on a globe. That's why flights from the US to Europe often go over Greenland!",
