      "your_hypothesis_which_parameter_forces": "Your hypothesis (which parameter forces the most stopovers?)",
      "e_g_v_formation_savings_only_pay_off_a": "e.g., V-formation savings only pay off above 1500 km because takeoff cost dominates...",
      "i_m_stuck_show_open_questions": "🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions",
      "open_questions_no_answer_key": "Open questions (no answer key)",
      "why_would_a_heavier_bird_necessarily_n": "Why would a heavier bird necessarily need MORE energy per km — what does that have to do with drag?",
      "a_10_m_s_headwind_doubles_the_work_wha": "A 10 m/s headwind doubles the work — what happens if half the journey has 10 m/s tailwind?",
      "why_is_v_formation_savings_about_22_wh": "Why is V-formation savings about 22%? What does it cost the lead bird?",
      "how_does_a_hummingbird_4_g_1500_km_gul": "How does a hummingbird (4 g, 1500 km Gulf of Mexico crossing) even survive its migration?",
      "i_can_explain_why_this_profile_yields_": "I can explain why this profile yields this energy state.",
      "explain_in_your_own_words": "Explain in your own words...",
      "inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a": "Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. Energy model is illustrative (drag ∝ m^0.67 / wingspan). V-formation savings vary ~10–30% across species and positions (Lissaman & Shollenberger 1970 theoretical upper bound; in-flight measurements include Weimerskirch et al. 2001 on pelicans and Portugal et al. 2014 on ibises). Widget uses 22% as a midpoint estimate. For real-world stopover ecology consult primary literature.",
      "v_formation_flying": "V-formation flying",
      "trailing_birds_catch_the_upwash_from_t": "Trailing birds catch the upwash from the bird ahead — 20–30% energy savings. Lead position rotates because the front bird does the most work.",
      "wind_currents_thermals": "Wind currents + thermals",
      "birds_read_pressure_gradients_we_canno": "Birds read pressure gradients we cannot feel. Updrafts, ridge lift, and thermal columns are how raptors fly hundreds of miles burning almost no calories.",
      "migration_routes_flyways": "Migration routes + flyways",
      "four_major_north_american_flyways_paci": "Four major North American flyways (Pacific, Central, Mississippi, Atlantic) channel billions of birds twice yearly. Maine sits at the top of the Atlantic Flyway.",
      "aerodynamics_of_bird_flight": "Aerodynamics of bird flight",
      "wing_shape_aspect_ratio_camber_tunes_l": "Wing shape (aspect ratio + camber) tunes lift vs drag. Soaring birds = high aspect ratio, slow wingbeat. Hummingbirds = low AR, 60+ Hz wingbeat.",
      "weather_navigation": "Weather + navigation",
      "birds_use_multiple_cues_simultaneously": "Birds use multiple cues simultaneously — sun compass, magnetic field via cryptochrome in the eye, star patterns, and learned landmarks. Robust against losing any single cue.",
      "v_formation_wind_currents_flyways_aero": "V-formation • Wind currents • Flyways • Aerodynamics • Navigation",
      "soaring_desc": "Long narrow wings maximize lift-to-drag ratio for effortless soaring. Slotted wingtip feathers reduce induced drag by spreading vortices. Eagles can soar for hours without a single flap, using thermals and ridge lift.",
      "flapping_desc": "Broad wings provide good lift at moderate speeds. Geese use powered flight with steady flapping for long-distance migration. A well-positioned trailing bird can cut its drag substantially via upwash exploitation (real flocks measure ~10–30% energy savings; ~65% is a theoretical per-position maximum).",
      "hovering_desc": "Hummingbird wings rotate at the shoulder, allowing a figure-8 stroke pattern that generates lift on both the downstroke AND upstroke. They can fly backwards, sideways, and hover in place. Wing beat: 50-80 times per second.",
      "speed_desc": "Swept-back pointed wings minimize drag at high speeds. During a stoop (dive), Peregrines tuck their wings to form a teardrop shape, reaching 240+ mph. A small tubercle on the beak disrupts airflow to prevent suffocation at speed.",
      "magnetic_desc": "Birds have magnetite crystals in their upper beaks connected to the trigeminal nerve. These crystals align with Earth's magnetic field like tiny compasses. Some species also have cryptochrome proteins in their eyes that may let them literally SEE magnetic field lines as colored overlays on their vision.",
      "stars_desc": "Nocturnal migrants (warblers, thrushes) use star patterns to navigate. Experiments in planetariums showed that birds orient to the rotation center of the night sky (near Polaris). Young birds learn star patterns during their first summer — they aren't born knowing them.",
      "sun_desc": "Birds track the sun's position and use an internal circadian clock to compensate for its movement across the sky. Experiments with clock-shifted birds (kept in artificially lit rooms) showed they navigate in predictably wrong directions, proving the sun-compass mechanism.",
      "landmarks_desc": "Experienced migrants follow visual landmarks: coastlines, mountain ranges, rivers, and highways. Pigeons even follow roads and make turns at intersections. This \"pilotage\" navigation is learned over multiple migration trips and passed down through flock experience.",
      "smell_desc": "Seabirds (petrels, albatrosses) navigate using olfactory maps of ocean scents. Dimethyl sulfide released by phytoplankton marks productive feeding areas. Homing pigeons also use smell — blocking their nostrils impairs their ability to find home.",
      "inherited_desc": "Some migration routes are genetically encoded. Young Cuckoos raised by foster parents of other species still migrate to the correct wintering grounds — a place they've never been, following a route they were never taught. The CLOCK gene and ADCYAP1 gene are linked to migratory restlessness.",
      "fat_reserve_chart": "Fat reserve chart showing the energy needed for the current flight.",
      "hypothesis_input": "Migration energy hypothesis",
      "explanation_input": "Migration energy explanation",
      "energy_inquiry_hint": "Test how wingspan, body mass, wind, formation, and distance combine to determine whether a migration leg is feasible.",
