      "2_00_mol_nh_17_03_g_mol_34_06_g_theore": "→ 2.00 mol NH₃ × 17.03 g/mol = 34.06 g (theoretical)",
      "phases_of_matter_phase_diagrams": "🧊 Phases of matter + phase diagrams",
      "a_phase_diagram_plots_phase_boundaries": "A phase diagram plots phase boundaries on a P (pressure) vs T (temperature) graph. Lines = phase transitions. Triple point: all three phases coexist. Critical point: liquid + gas become indistinguishable (supercritical fluid).",
      "water_is_unusual": "💧 Water is unusual: ",
      "ice_is_less_dense_than_liquid_water_ic": "Ice is LESS dense than liquid water (ice floats). Most substances: solid denser than liquid. Hydrogen bonding gives ice its open crystal structure.",
      "sublimation": "🌬 Sublimation: ",
      "solid_gas_without_going_through_liquid": "Solid → gas without going through liquid (CO₂ dry ice; iodine at room temp). Reverse: deposition.",
      "chemical_equilibrium_le_chatelier": "⇌ Chemical equilibrium + Le Chatelier",
      "reversible_reactions_reach_dynamic_equ": "Reversible reactions reach dynamic equilibrium when forward rate = reverse rate. Concentrations stop changing (but reactions keep going both ways). Keq = product of [products]^coefficients / product of [reactants]^coefficients.",
      "le_chatelier_s_principle": "Le Chatelier's principle",
      "if_a_stress_is_applied_to_a_system_at_": "If a stress is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system shifts to relieve that stress. Predict the direction of shift to make sense of how rxns respond to changes.",
      "reaction_kinetics_what_controls_speed": "⏱ Reaction kinetics — what controls speed",
      "kinetics_how_fast_thermodynamics_wheth": "Kinetics = how fast. Thermodynamics = whether possible. A reaction can be very favorable (large negative ΔG) but slow (high Ea). Catalysts and conditions tune kinetics.",
      "arrhenius_equation": "📐 Arrhenius equation",
      "k_a_exp_ea_rt": "k = A · exp(−Ea / RT)",
      "k_rate_constant_a_collision_frequency_": "k = rate constant · A = collision frequency factor · Ea = activation energy · R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) · T = absolute temperature (K). Plot ln(k) vs 1/T; slope = −Ea/R.",
      "thermodynamics_what_s_spontaneous": "🔥 Thermodynamics — what's spontaneous",
