      "decoder": "Decoder",
      "all_five_challenges_decoded": "✓ All five challenges decoded",
      "1_atp_mitochondria": "1. ATP & Mitochondria",
      "2_three_energy_systems": "2. Three Energy Systems",
      "3_where_energy_goes": "3. Where Energy Goes",
      "atp_your_cellular_energy_currency": "ATP — your cellular energy currency",
      "every_cell_holds_50g_of_atp_and_recycl": "Every cell holds ~50g of ATP and recycles it ~1,000× per day.",
      "three_energy_systems_run_side_by_side": "Three energy systems run side by side",
      "pcr_glycolysis_oxidative_each_peaks_at": "PCr · glycolysis · oxidative — each peaks at a different duration.",
      "where_your_energy_actually_goes_at_res": "Where your energy actually goes at rest",
      "brain_20_of_resting_energy_at_only_2_o": "Brain ≈ 20% of resting energy at only ~2% of body weight.",
      "constantly_processing_nutrients_detoxi": "Constantly processing nutrients, detoxifying, making proteins.",
      "even_at_rest_muscles_maintain_tone_and": "Even at rest, muscles maintain tone and run baseline metabolism.",
      "neurons_fire_constantly_brain_uses_20_": "Neurons fire constantly. Brain uses ~20% of resting energy despite being ~2% of body weight.",
      "beats_100_000_times_day_never_stops": "Beats ~100,000 times/day, never stops.",
      "filter_your_entire_blood_volume_60_tim": "Filter your entire blood volume ~60 times/day.",
      "skin_gut_immune_cells_fat_tissue_nervo": "Skin, gut, immune cells, fat tissue, nervous system.",
      "slightly_below_bmr_brain_and_organs_ar": "Slightly below BMR. Brain and organs are still busy.",
      "bmr_baseline_plus_a_tiny_bit_for_postu": "BMR baseline plus a tiny bit for posture.",
      "most_accessible_activity_for_nearly_an": "Most accessible activity for nearly any body.",
      "joint_friendly_sustained_exercise": "Joint-friendly, sustained exercise.",
      "aerobic_system_in_full_use_oxidative_g": "Aerobic system in full use; oxidative + glycolysis.",
      "whole_body_low_impact_high_energy": "Whole-body, low-impact, high-energy.",
      "phosphocreatine_anaerobic_glycolysis_c": "Phosphocreatine + anaerobic glycolysis. Cannot sustain.",
      "energy_metabolism_2": "Energy & Metabolism",
      "how_your_body_actually_makes_and_uses_": "How your body actually makes and uses energy",
      "this_module_covers_the_biology_of_ener": "This module covers the BIOLOGY of energy — molecules, cells, organ systems. Numbers like \"BMR\" and \"kcal/min\" are descriptive (this is what your body is doing), not prescriptive (this is your daily target). Different bodies need different amounts. Growth, illness, sleep quality, weather, and life stage all change the numbers. None of this is a goal to hit.",
      "energy_metabolism_sections": "Energy metabolism sections",
      "atp_your_cellular_energy_currency_2": "ATP — your cellular energy currency",
      "every_cell_in_your_body_runs_on_a_tiny": "Every cell in your body runs on a tiny molecule called ATP (adenosine triphosphate). When ATP gives up one phosphate group, it becomes ADP and releases energy your cells use to do work — contract muscles, fire neurons, build proteins, push molecules across membranes.",
      "releases_energy": "releases energy",
      "adp_pi": "ADP + Pi",
      "recharged_in_mitochondria": "recharged in mitochondria",
      "your_body_holds_only_50_g_of_atp_at_an": "Your body holds only ~50 g of ATP at any moment, but recycles it constantly: each ATP molecule cycles ATP → ADP → ATP about 1,000+ times per day. Total ATP turnover roughly equals your body weight per day. The recycling happens in tiny organelles called mitochondria, which use oxygen and food (glucose, fatty acids) to charge ADP back into ATP.",
      "mitochondria_the_cellular_powerhouses": "Mitochondria — the cellular powerhouses",
      "mitochondria_are_tiny_organelles_insid": "Mitochondria are tiny organelles inside almost every cell of your body. They take glucose (from carbs) or fatty acids (from fats), combine them with oxygen, and release CO₂ + water — capturing the energy in ATP along the way.",
      "glucose_6_o_6_co_6_h_o_30_32_atp": "Glucose + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + ~30-32 ATP",
      "this_is_cellular_respiration_the_chemi": "(this is cellular respiration — the chemistry that powers you right now)",
      "cells_with_high_energy_demand_have": "Cells with high energy demand have ",
      "lots_of_mitochondria": "lots of mitochondria",
      "heart_muscle_5_000_per_cell_liver_brai": ": heart muscle (~5,000 per cell), liver, brain. Endurance training increases mitochondrial density in your muscles — that's a big part of why trained athletes can sustain effort longer.",
      "three_energy_systems_run_side_by_side_2": "Three energy systems run side by side",
      "your_body_has_three_different_ways_to_": "Your body has three different ways to make ATP. They're all running constantly, but the dominant system depends on how hard and how long you're working.",
      "phosphocreatine_pcr": "Phosphocreatine (PCr)",
      "anaerobic_glycolysis": "Anaerobic glycolysis",
      "oxidative_aerobic": "Oxidative (aerobic)",
      "atp_yield": "ATP yield: ",
      "all_three_systems_run_all_the_time": "All three systems run all the time. ",
      "they_overlap_a_1500m_race_shifts_from_": "They overlap. A 1500m race shifts from PCr → anaerobic → aerobic across the four minutes. Endurance training boosts the oxidative system; sprint training boosts the anaerobic. Both adapt with practice.",
      "where_your_energy_actually_goes": "Where your energy actually goes",
      "even_when_you_re_resting_your_body_use": "Even when you're resting, your body uses energy. The minimum to keep your heart beating, brain firing, lungs breathing, kidneys filtering, and core temperature stable is called Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Most adolescents have a BMR somewhere between 1,200 and 1,800 kcal/day — the wide range is normal biological variation.",
      "resting_energy_distribution_by_organ": "Resting energy distribution by organ",
      "these_percentages_describe_a_healthy_a": "These percentages describe a healthy adult at rest. Children and adolescents typically have a brain that uses an even larger share (up to 50% in young kids) because of growth and learning.",
      "activity_adds_energy_use_on_top_of_bmr": "Activity adds energy use on top of BMR",
      "these_are_descriptive_averages_actual_": "These are descriptive averages — actual energy use varies with body size, fitness, and how hard you're going.",
      "important_framing": "Important framing",
      "your_body_needs_at_least_your_bmr_wort": "Your body needs at least your BMR worth of energy to keep its essential systems working. Eating well below BMR for an extended time forces the body to slow metabolism, break down muscle, weaken immunity, and impair concentration. The goal isn't to \"minimize calories\" — it's to give your body what it needs to do its job. If anything in this module triggers worry about how much you're eating, please talk with a trusted adult, your doctor, or NEDA (1-800-931-2237).",
