      "sources_institute_of_medicine_2005_die": "Sources: Institute of Medicine (2005) Dietary Reference Intakes for Water, still cited by AAP and NIH ODS. Activity adder from NATA 2017 Position Statement on Fluid Replacement.",
      "the_8_point_urine_color_check": "The 8-point urine-color check",
      "look_at_your_urine_the_next_time_you_u": "Look at your urine the next time you use the bathroom. Pick the color closest to what you saw. The Armstrong 8-point scale is widely used in athletic training and clinical hydration assessment.",
      "urine_color": "Urine color",
      "other_body_signals_worth_tracking": "Other body signals worth tracking",
      "thirst_is_a_useful_late_signal_not_an_": "Thirst is a useful late signal, not an early one — by the time you feel thirsty you are usually already at 1-2% deficit.",
      "lightheaded_standing_up_heart_pounding": "Lightheaded standing up, heart pounding at rest, headache without other cause: classic mild-moderate dehydration in clinical guides.",
      "foggy_attention_slower_math_irritabili": "Foggy attention, slower math, irritability: documented in studies as effects of mild dehydration in adolescents.",
      "dry_lips_and_dry_mouth_are_real_signal": "Dry lips and dry mouth are real signals — but skin \"tenting\" (slow snap-back) only shows up at moderate-to-severe dehydration.",
      "persistent_dark_urine_despite_drinking": "Persistent dark urine despite drinking water? See a clinician — could be liver, medication, or blood-related.",
      "if_you_find_yourself_using_water_to_fe": "If you find yourself using water to feel \"full\" or override hunger, that is a body-cue mismatch worth talking about with a trusted adult. NEDA helpline: 1-800-931-2237.",
      "what_is_in_8_oz_of": "What is in 8 oz of...",
      "sodium_and_added_sugar_matter_for_hydr": "Sodium and added sugar matter for hydration efficacy AND for daily nutrient targets. AAP guidance: added sugar under 25 g/day for ages 2-18; sodium under ~2,300 mg/day for adolescents.",
