      "don_quixote_part_1": "Don Quixote, Part 1",
      "king_james_bible_authorized_version": "King James Bible (Authorized Version)",
      "philosophical_transactions": "Philosophical Transactions",
      "publick_occurrences": "Publick Occurrences",
      "common_sense": "Common Sense",
      "falmouth_gazette_and_weekly_advertiser": "Falmouth Gazette and Weekly Advertiser",
      "cherokee_phoenix_cherokee_syllabary_in": "Cherokee Phoenix (Cherokee syllabary in print)",
      "steam_powered_cylinder_press": "Steam-powered cylinder press",
      "maine_becomes_a_state_missouri_comprom": "Maine becomes a state (Missouri Compromise)",
      "uncle_tom_s_cabin": "Uncle Tom's Cabin",
      "the_narrative_most_u_s_textbooks_tell_": "The narrative most U.S. textbooks tell is \"Gutenberg invented printing in 1450.\" That is incomplete. Gutenberg invented the system that scaled printing for European alphabets in Europe. Printing as a concept and a practice is older and broader. The honest version names where else printing happened, and why those traditions did not produce the same explosive scale.",
      "what_happened_2": "What happened: ",
      "why_it_did_not_scale_like_mainz": "Why it did not scale like Mainz: ",
      "the_right_framing_gutenberg_solved_a_s": "The right framing: Gutenberg solved a specific problem for a specific writing system at a specific moment, and that solution scaled fast. He did not invent the human idea of duplicating texts. Crediting him as \"the\" inventor of printing erases hundreds of years of innovation in other parts of the world. The honest sentence is \"Gutenberg developed the system that made movable-type printing economically viable for European alphabets.\"",
      "the_falmouth_gazette_1785_was_the_firs": "The Falmouth Gazette (1785) was the first newspaper printed in what would become Maine. But the land had a sophisticated information culture for thousands of years before colonial printers arrived. Naming this honestly is part of the local-history thread.",
      "the_wabanaki_confederacy_penobscot_pas": "The Wabanaki Confederacy: Penobscot, Passamaquoddy, Maliseet, Mi'kmaq, and Abenaki nations. Together they had occupied what is now Maine, the Maritimes, and northern New England for at least 10,000 years.",
      "oral_traditions_backed_by_trained_memo": "Oral traditions backed by trained memory keepers. Stories, treaty histories, place-names, plant knowledge, navigation, and seasonal cycles were carried by individuals who specialized in remembering. Oral does not mean unreliable; specialist memorizers were checked against each other and against material artifacts.",
      "wampum_belts_shell_beads_strung_in_pat": "Wampum belts (shell beads strung in patterns) recorded treaties, alliances, and historical events. A wampum keeper could read a belt the way a European could read a manuscript. Some surviving wampum belts are 400+ years old and still legible to trained readers. Maine museums and tribal cultural centers hold examples.",
      "mi_kmaq_and_other_wabanaki_nations_use": "Mi'kmaq and other Wabanaki nations used inscribed birchbark for some communications, especially religious and historical material. Some scrolls survive in museum collections; the inscription technique predates colonial contact.",
      "colonial_missionary_presses_began_prin": "Colonial missionary presses began printing in Wabanaki languages in the 1600s. The Eliot Indian Bible (1663, Massachusett-language) is the most famous example, set in Cambridge MA by John Eliot with native-language consultants. Print-in-Wabanaki-languages and oral-tradition-in-Wabanaki-languages coexisted for centuries; both continue today.",
      "for_maine_students_the_abbe_museum_bar": "For Maine students: the Abbe Museum (Bar Harbor), the Maine Indian Basketmakers Alliance, and tribal cultural centers in Indian Township and Pleasant Point are working sources today. The Penobscot Nation Library (Indian Island) holds materials many state archives do not. Treat these as living institutions, not just historical references.",
      "approximate_cumulative_book_production": "Approximate cumulative book production in Europe (Buringh & van Zanden, \"Charting the Rise of the West\"):",
      "the_gutenberg_bible_was_first_but_the_": "The Gutenberg Bible was first. But the next 38 years produced an extraordinary variety of \"firsts\" as printers tried out what the new technology could do beyond the Bible.",
      "gutenberg_bible_2": "Gutenberg Bible",
      "mainz_psalter": "Mainz Psalter",
      "catholicon": "Catholicon",
      "der_edelstein": "Der Edelstein",
      "cicero_de_oratore": "Cicero, De Oratore",
      "mentelin_bible": "Mentelin Bible",
      "augustine_de_civitate_dei": "Augustine, De Civitate Dei",
      "recuyell_of_the_historyes_of_troye": "Recuyell of the Historyes of Troye",
      "dante_divine_comedy": "Dante, Divine Comedy",
      "mainz_mainzer_pilgerfahrt": "Mainz Mainzer Pilgerfahrt",
      "nuremberg_chronicle": "Nuremberg Chronicle",
      "within_40_years_of_the_gutenberg_bible": "Within 40 years of the Gutenberg Bible, every major category of book we still print had at least one printed exemplar: scripture, scholarship, classics, dictionaries, vernacular literature, illustrated books, reference, travel writing, popular illustration. The press did not just produce more books; it discovered what books could be.",
      "each_marker_is_a_moment_when_print_ena": "Each marker is a moment when print enabled something that would otherwise have stayed local, slow, or impossible. Click to learn what changed.",
      "close_detail": "Close detail",
      "primary_source_digital_facsimile": "→ Primary source / digital facsimile",
      "click_any_marker_above_to_read_about_t": "Click any marker above to read about the event.",
      "falmouth_gazette_1785": "Falmouth Gazette (1785) ",
      "was_published_in_what_is_now_portland_": "was published in what is now Portland, less than two miles from King Middle. It helped build the case for separating Maine from Massachusetts (achieved 1820). ",
      "uncle_tom_s_cabin_1851": "Uncle Tom's Cabin (1851) ",
      "was_written_by_harriet_beecher_stowe_i": "was written by Harriet Beecher Stowe in Brunswick, Maine — a 25-mile drive from Portland — while her husband taught at Bowdoin College. 300,000 copies in its first US year, the most-printed novel of the 19th century. The press in Mainz reached the kitchen table in Maine.",
