      "a_student_says_gutenberg_invented_the_": "A student says \"Gutenberg invented the printing press all by himself.\" What is a more historically accurate framing?",
      "thomas_b_wait_benjamin_titcomb": "Thomas B. Wait & Benjamin Titcomb",
      "falmouth_district_of_maine_january_1_1": "Falmouth, District of Maine · January 1, 1785",
      "wait_and_titcomb_founded_the": "Wait and Titcomb founded the ",
      "falmouth_gazette_and_weekly_advertiser_2": "Falmouth Gazette and Weekly Advertiser",
      "the_first_newspaper_printed_in_what_wo": ", the first newspaper printed in what would become Maine. They set up a press in Falmouth (now Portland) thirty-five years before Maine statehood, when Maine was still the District of Maine inside Massachusetts. Their press ran from a small shop on Fore Street.",
      "why_this_matters_a_frontier_newspaper_": "Why this matters: a frontier newspaper in 1785 was a political act. The Falmouth Gazette covered the statehood debate, advertised land sales, reprinted Boston and London news arriving by ship, and gave coastal Maine its first sustained source of printed information. Without local presses, news traveled at the speed of a horse from Boston.",
      "where_the_press_sat": "Where the press sat",
      "fore_street_falmouth_now_portland_bloc": "Fore Street, Falmouth (now Portland). Block walkable today from King Middle.",
      "successor_papers": "Successor papers",
      "cumberland_gazette_1786_eastern_argus_": "Cumberland Gazette (1786), Eastern Argus (1803), and on to the modern ",
      "portland_press_herald": "Portland Press Herald",
      "a_240_year_chain": ". A 240-year chain.",
      "field_trip": "Field trip",
      "maine_historical_society_and_the_wadsw": "Maine Historical Society and the Wadsworth-Longfellow House (Congress Street) hold early Falmouth Gazette issues.",
      "a_handful_of_facts_that_did_not_fit_th": "A handful of facts that did not fit the main stories above, but are too good not to know.",
      "gutenberg_s_actual_surname_was_gensfle": "Gutenberg's actual surname was Gensfleisch.",
      "gutenberg_came_from_the_family_estate_": "\"Gutenberg\" came from the family estate \"zum Gutenberg\" in Mainz. Johannes Gensfleisch zur Laden zum Gutenberg is the full name. We remember the street he lived on, not his birth name.",
      "the_aldine_dolphin_and_anchor_was_so_h": "The Aldine dolphin-and-anchor was so heavily pirated that Aldus published a guide to spotting fakes.",
      "aldine_books_were_bestsellers_across_e": "Aldine books were bestsellers across Europe in the 1500s. Lyonnaise and Venetian rivals copied the mark and sold lower-quality knockoffs. Aldus printed a small pamphlet describing the precise proportions of his mark so buyers could check for themselves. The first published anti-counterfeiting guide.",
      "charlotte_guillard_outlived_two_husban": "Charlotte Guillard outlived two husbands (both printers) and ran her shop for 40 years.",
      "she_inherited_the_press_from_her_first": "She inherited the press from her first husband, kept it through her second marriage to another printer, and continued running it for decades after his death. Over her career the Guillard press produced more than 150 substantial scholarly books. Many bore her own preface or note. Standard 19th-century histories did not mention her.",
      "peter_sch_ffer_printed_the_first_multi": "Peter Schöffer printed the first multicolor book using the press.",
      "the_1457_mainz_psalter_fust_sch_ffer_u": "The 1457 Mainz Psalter (Fust & Schöffer) used a complicated two-color printing technique: the body type in black, the large initial capitals printed in red and blue on the same impression. This required printing each page through the press in multiple passes with the type inked in different colors. Most scholars consider it among the most beautifully printed books ever made.",
      "benjamin_franklin_ran_away_from_his_ap": "Benjamin Franklin ran away from his apprenticeship at age 17.",
      "his_older_brother_james_was_a_boston_p": "His older brother James was a Boston printer who took Ben on as an apprentice at age 12. Five years in, Ben fled to Philadelphia rather than finish the term. The legal apprenticeship contract he broke is the same kind of contract you read above. He spent the rest of his life as a printer first and a politician second.",
      "johannes_gutenberg_2": "Johannes Gutenberg",
      "you_care_about_getting_things_exactly_": "You care about getting things exactly right. You would have been the person who tested 27 alloy ratios before committing to one. You measure twice and cut once. Your weakness: sometimes you forget that \"good enough and shipped\" beats \"perfect and abandoned.\"",
      "aldus_manutius_2": "Aldus Manutius",
      "you_care_about_making_things_accessibl": "You care about making things accessible. You would have invented the paperback because you were sick of seeing classics locked up in expensive folios. You think reach matters at least as much as quality. Your weakness: sometimes you simplify what should stay complex.",
      "robert_estienne": "Robert Estienne",
      "you_care_about_accuracy_and_citation_y": "You care about accuracy and citation. You would have invented Bible verse numbers because you got tired of saying \"the bit near the end of Matthew.\" Your reference works become the standard everyone else cites. Your weakness: you can lose readers in the apparatus.",
      "benjamin_franklin_2": "Benjamin Franklin",
      "you_care_about_impact_on_the_world_you": "You care about impact on the world. You would have run a newspaper, a postal service, and a revolution at the same time. You believe a well-aimed pamphlet beats a brilliant treatise. Your weakness: you sometimes choose persuasion over precision.",
      "a_quick_4_question_quiz_pick_the_answe": "A quick 4-question quiz. Pick the answer that feels truest to you, even if more than one fits. At the end you will see which 1450-1750 printer you most resemble — and what modern roles that maps to.",
