      "eric_foner_tom_paine_and_revolutionary": "Eric Foner, \"Tom Paine and Revolutionary America\" (1976)",
      "possible_angles_human_nature_does_not_": "Possible angles: human nature does not change; new tech always disrupts existing institutions; the arguments are templates we reach for whenever something powerful arrives.",
      "no_clean_answer_worth_noticing_the_int": "No clean answer. Worth noticing: the internet is ~30 years old, social media is ~20, generative AI is ~3. We are early in a 150-year settling.",
      "real_concerns_usually_cite_specific_ha": "Real concerns usually: cite specific harms with evidence, propose specific interventions, distinguish populations affected. Moral panics usually: invoke vague civilizational decline, target whole technologies rather than uses, assume the worst about new generations.",
      "probably_right_some_workers_will_be_di": "Probably right: some workers WILL be displaced; some institutions WILL be disrupted. Probably wrong: humanity will not lose the ability to think. The hard work is sorting which is which.",
      "open_question_worth_real_discussion_so": "Open question worth real discussion. Some candidates students might raise: leaded gasoline, asbestos, certain social-media features. The honest answer is that \"should have\" requires hindsight that critics did not have.",
      "these_are_not_invented_or_exaggerated_": "These are not invented or exaggerated. Each was a genuine, documented concern voiced by someone in the 1450-1600 period. The voice attribution names the actual source where the concern was raised.",
      "1450_fear": "1450 fear",
      "map_the_1450_concern_to_its_2026_echo_": "Map the 1450 concern to its 2026 echo. The technology changed; the argument template did not. Read each row across, then ask yourself: same fear, or different?",
      "discussion_hint": "Discussion hint: ",
      "a_1500_scholar_argues_that_printed_boo": "A 1500 scholar argues that printed books are inferior to manuscript copies because printers cut corners on accuracy. A 2026 scholar argues the same thing about AI-generated text. What do these two arguments share, and what makes them different?",
