      "modern": "Modern",
      "a_1650_hornbook_was_made_by_printing_t": "A 1650 hornbook was made by: printing the lesson sheet on paper or parchment; mounting it on a wooden paddle (oak or beech); cutting a thin sheet of transparent cow horn to size; nailing the horn over the print with small brass tacks. The result was durable enough to last a child's entire primary education. By the 1700s mass-produced hornbooks were sold for a few pence in market towns; by the early 1800s the form was largely replaced by printed primers like the New England Primer.",
      "for_350_years_1500_1850_chapbooks_were": "For ~350 years (1500-1850), chapbooks were the only books many people could afford. They circulated fairy tales + ballads across continents. They were beloved by Wordsworth + Coleridge + Robert Louis Stevenson as childhood reading. Many authors' first published works appeared in chapbook form. They are the direct ancestor of mass-market paperback fiction.",
      "cooperative_children_s_book_center_uw_": "Cooperative Children's Book Center (UW-Madison)",
      "we_need_diverse_books": "We Need Diverse Books",
      "american_library_association_newbery_c": "American Library Association — Newbery + Caldecott",
      "maine_reads_maine_state_library_childr": "Maine Reads / Maine State Library children's lit collection",
