      "soft_2_3_mm_fringe_on_the_back_edge_ab": "Soft 2-3 mm fringe on the back edge absorbs the trailing-edge turbulent vortex sound. This is the largest contributor — most aerodynamic noise comes from the trailing edge.",
      "3_velvety_dorsal_surface": "3. Velvety dorsal surface",
      "tiny_velvet_like_pile_0_5_mm_on_the_up": "Tiny velvet-like pile (~0.5 mm) on the upper feather surface dampens feather-on-feather friction noise during wing folding/unfolding.",
      "frequency_shift_the_actual_mechanism": "🔊 Frequency shift — the actual mechanism",
      "owl_wing_noise_peaks_at_16_khz_hawk_wi": "Owl wing noise peaks at ~16 kHz. Hawk wing noise peaks at ~2-4 kHz. The owl achieves silence not by reducing total noise — that's a small effect — but by shifting the dominant frequency range out of where prey ears are most sensitive. A mouse hears 2-4 kHz acutely (rustling, footsteps) but is much less sensitive at 16 kHz.",
      "wing_noise_spectrum_hawk_vs_owl_vs_mou": "📈 Wing Noise Spectrum — Hawk vs Owl vs Mouse Hearing",
      "x_axis_frequency_khz_y_axis_relative_i": "X-axis: frequency (kHz). Y-axis: relative intensity / sensitivity. Hawk wing noise (red) peaks right in the mouse's most-sensitive range. Owl wing noise (violet) is shifted up out of that range. The frequency shift IS the silence mechanism.",
      "frequency_spectrum_chart": "Frequency spectrum chart",
