      "quiz1_q": "What happens to pitch when frequency increases?",
      "quiz1_fb_goes_down": "Backwards — MORE vibrations per second means HIGHER pitch. Slide frequency up with Play Sound on and listen to it climb.",
      "quiz1_fb_stays_same": "Pitch IS your ear’s perception of frequency — change one and you have changed the other.",
      "quiz1_fb_disappears": "Sound only disappears when AMPLITUDE reaches zero. Frequency changes the pitch, not whether it exists.",
      "quiz2_q": "What does amplitude control?",
      "quiz2_fb_speed": "Wave speed is set by the MEDIUM (air, water, string tension) — a louder shout does not arrive any sooner.",
      "quiz2_fb_color": "For light, FREQUENCY sets the color. Amplitude sets brightness — and for sound, loudness.",
      "quiz2_fb_direction": "Direction comes from the source and reflections. Amplitude is the wave’s height — how much energy it carries.",
      "quiz3_q": "What is superposition?",
      "quiz3_fb_canceling": "Canceling is only ONE special case (destructive). Superposition is the general rule: overlapping waves ADD their displacements — sometimes bigger, sometimes smaller.",
      "quiz3_fb_reflecting": "Reflection is a wave bouncing off a boundary. Superposition is what happens when two waves OVERLAP — try the Ripple Tank.",
      "quiz3_fb_stopping": "Waves pass straight through each other and continue unchanged — they never stop each other.",
      "quiz4_q": "Destructive interference occurs when...",
      "quiz4_fb_peaks": "Peak-on-peak is CONSTRUCTIVE interference — the wave gets bigger. Destruction needs opposites: peak + trough = flat.",
      "quiz4_fb_stop": "The waves keep traveling — they only cancel at the spots where they overlap out of phase. Watch the dark bands in the Ripple Tank.",
      "quiz4_fb_doubles": "Doubling is constructive interference. Destructive SUBTRACTS — a peak fills a trough.",
      "quiz5_q": "Sound is what type of wave?",
      "quiz5_fb_transverse": "Transverse waves wiggle PERPENDICULAR to travel (light, a guitar string). Air molecules push and pull ALONG the direction of travel — switch to Longitudinal mode and watch the compressions.",
      "quiz5_fb_circular": "Water ripples spread in circles, but that describes the pattern on the surface, not the wave type.",
      "quiz5_fb_standing": "Standing waves are trapped between boundaries. Traveling sound is free — it only forms standing waves inside pipes and rooms.",
      "quiz6_q": "At a node of a standing wave, the displacement is:",
      "quiz6_fb_maximum": "Maximum displacement is the ANTInode. Nodes are the still points — in Standing mode, watch the spots where the string never moves.",
      "quiz6_fb_half": "Nodes are perfectly still — zero, not half. Points BETWEEN node and antinode oscillate at partial amplitude.",
      "quiz6_fb_random": "Standing waves are perfectly ordered — the nodes stay locked in place. That is exactly why they are called STANDING.",
      "quiz7_q": "The speed of a wave equals:",
      "quiz7_fb_amp_freq": "Amplitude carries ENERGY, not speed. v = f × λ: waves per second × length of each wave = distance per second.",
      "quiz7_fb_period_amp": "Period is just 1/frequency and amplitude is height — multiplying them gives nothing physical. v = f × λ.",
      "quiz7_fb_none": "One of them IS right: v = f × λ — how many waves pass per second times how long each one is.",
      "tour_step1": "Welcome! Drag the Amplitude and Frequency sliders to shape your wave — or focus the wave itself and use the arrow keys. Space pauses.",
